<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Inventions That Changed the World</h1><p class="ws-lede">From the wheel and cuneiform to printing, steam power and the electric telegraph.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/inventions-that-changed-the-world">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 3 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>James Watt</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Johannes Gutenberg</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Cai Lun</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Cai Lun</li><li>Johannes Gutenberg</li><li>James Watt</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1736–1819</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 62–121</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1400–1468</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Magnetic compass</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Microscope</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Movable type</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Steam engine</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Submarine</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Wheel</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Miniature clay wheels from north of the Black Sea, from before 4000 BCE, are the oldest indirect evidence of wheeled movement; the spoked wheel later allowed swifter war chariots.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Johannes Gutenberg</b>: &#8220;In the fifteenth century Johannes Gutenberg developed printing with movable metal type in Europe, and his press transformed the spread of knowledge there.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Telegraph</b>: &#8220;Developed in the nineteenth century, the telegraph sent messages by electrical signals along wires, and submarine cables linked Britain with India, Australia and other parts of its empire.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;From the wheel and cuneiform to printing, steam power and the electric telegraph.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/inventions-that-changed-the-world. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Inventions That Changed the World</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Cai Lun <span>born c. 62 CE</span></li><li>Johannes Gutenberg <span>born c. 1400 CE</span></li><li>James Watt <span>born 1736 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Cai Lun <span>2: c. 62–121</span></li><li>Johannes Gutenberg <span>3: c. 1400–1468</span></li><li>James Watt <span>1: 1736–1819</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Magnetic compass</dt><dd>The magnetic compass is a navigational instrument that uses a magnetised needle to indicate direction.</dd><dt>Microscope</dt><dd>A microscope is an instrument for examining objects too small to be seen by the naked eye; the earliest known compound microscopes, which combine an objective lens near the specimen with an eyepiece, appeared in Europe…</dd><dt>Movable type</dt><dd>Movable type is a printing system using individual, reusable characters that can be arranged to form texts.</dd><dt>Steam engine</dt><dd>A steam engine is a heat engine that does mechanical work with steam, usually by pushing a piston in a cylinder; the Spanish inventor Jerónimo de Ayanz patented the first steam-powered water pump, for draining mines…</dd><dt>Submarine</dt><dd>A submarine is a watercraft capable of independent operation underwater; the first submersible of which reliable information exists, propelled by oars, was built in 1620 by Cornelis Drebbel, a Dutchman in the service…</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
